So the stack becomes Java 21 + Spring Boot 3 + Kafka + PostgreSQL on the enterprise/backend side, with Python + FastAPI + LangGraph + Pandas/NumPy/PyTorch on the AI and quant side.

Java can own APIs such as:

POST /api/research
GET  /api/research/{id}
GET  /api/strategies/{id}
GET  /api/backtests/{id}
GET  /api/reports/{id}

For example, a user submits:

{
  "query": "Find a momentum strategy for NVDA",
  "startDate": "2018-01-01",
  "endDate": "2026-01-01",
  "maxDrawdown": 0.20
}

Spring Boot validates and persists the request, then publishes something like:

quant.research.requested

The Python LangGraph service consumes it and runs:

Market Data
    ↓
RAG Research
    ↓
Strategy Generation
    ↓
Backtest
    ↓
Risk
    ↓
Evaluation
    ↓
Report

Then the result comes back through Kafka:

quant.research.completed

That gives you a much better Forward Deployed Engineer story than a Python-only demo because you can explain enterprise integration, distributed systems, event-driven architecture, AI agents, RAG, tool calling, quantitative computation, observability, and deployment in one project.




I would therefore build it in two layers:

Phase 1: Python/FastAPI/LangGraph first so we get the actual AI Quant Agent working.

Phase 2: Introduce Java Spring Boot + Kafka as the production integration layer.

The final portfolio stack would be:

React + Java 21 + Spring Boot 3 + Kafka + Python + FastAPI + LangGraph + RAG + PostgreSQL/pgvector + Redis + Pandas/NumPy + PyTorch + Docker + AWS.

That is the version I would use for your FDE portfolio.